[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m [response] To determine how many crab pots have three different colored balloons attached, we need to find the pots that are marked with a red balloon (every 4th pot), a blue balloon (every 6th pot), and a yellow balloon (every 10th pot). This means we need to find the pots that are multiples of 4, 6, and 10 simultaneously. The least common multiple (LCM) of these numbers will give us the interval at which a pot is marked with all three colors.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m First, we find the LCM of 4, 6, and 10. We start by finding the prime factorizations of each number:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m - \(4 = 2^2\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m - \(6 = 2 \times 3\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m - \(10 = 2 \times 5\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m - The highest power of 2 is \(2^2\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m - The highest power of 3 is \(3\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m - The highest power of 5 is \(5\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m So, the LCM is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ \text{LCM} = 2^2 \times 3 \times 5 = 4 \times 3 \times 5 = 60 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
