[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m First, we find the smallest three-digit number divisible by 13. The smallest three-digit number is 100. We divide 100 by 13 to find the smallest integer quotient:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 100 \div 13 \approx 7.692 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Rounding up to the nearest whole number, we get 8. So, the smallest three-digit number divisible by 13 is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 13 \times 8 = 104 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Next, we find the largest three-digit number divisible by 13. The largest three-digit number is 999. We divide 999 by 13 to find the largest integer quotient:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 999 \div 13 \approx 76.846 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Rounding down to the nearest whole number, we get 76. So, the largest three-digit number divisible by 13 is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 13 \times 76 = 988 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Now, we need to count the number of terms in the arithmetic sequence starting from 104 and ending at 988 with a common difference of 13. The number of terms \( n \) in an arithmetic sequence can be found using the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m where \( a_n \) is the last term, \( a_1 \) is the first term, \( d \) is the common difference, and \( n \) is the number of terms. Plugging in the values, we get:
