[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m The smallest three-digit number is 100. To find the smallest three-digit number divisible by 13, we divide 100 by 13 and round up to the nearest whole number:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 100 \div 13 \approx 7.692 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Rounding up, we get 8. So, the smallest three-digit number divisible by 13 is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 13 \times 8 = 104 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m The largest three-digit number is 999. To find the largest three-digit number divisible by 13, we divide 999 by 13 and round down to the nearest whole number:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 999 \div 13 \approx 76.846 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Rounding down, we get 76. So, the largest three-digit number divisible by 13 is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m \[ 13 \times 76 = 988 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Now, we need to count the number of terms in the arithmetic sequence starting from 104 and ending at 988 with a common difference of 13. The number of terms \( n \) in an arithmetic sequence can be found using the formula:
