[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m For $0^\circ< x < 180^\circ$, $\sin x$ ranges from $0$ to $1$, excluding $0$ because $\sin x$ is strictly positive in this interval. Thus, $\sin x \in (0, 1]$.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Next, we consider $\sqrt{\sin x}$. Since $\sin x$ ranges from $0$ to $1$, $\sqrt{\sin x}$ will range from $\sqrt{0}$ to $\sqrt{1}$, excluding $0$. Therefore, $\sqrt{\sin x} \in (0, 1]$.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Now, we apply the logarithm base $2$ to $\sqrt{\sin x}$. The function $\log_2 (\sqrt{\sin x})$ will take values from $\log_2 (0^+)$ to $\log_2 (1)$, where $\log_2 (0^+)$ approaches $-\infty$ as $\sqrt{\sin x}$ approaches $0$ from the positive side, and $\log_2 (1) = 0$.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2059637)[0m Therefore, the range of $y=\log_2 (\sqrt{\sin x})$ for $0^\circ< x < 180^\circ$ is $(-\infty, 0]$. 
